of the way. There's lots of confusion about like,&nbsp;
how does inflation eventually go away? And one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:25.920
the realities about inflation is that sustained&nbsp;
inflation will eventually reduce inflation. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:31.120
I'm going to explain to you how that works.&nbsp;
But when people talk about markets and cycles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.200
this is actually what we mean. One of the best&nbsp;
there are many examples of markets that don't work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.840
or are kind of pie in the sky fantasy. They don't&nbsp;
relate to how the real world works when it comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:48.960
to inflation. Ultimately resolving inflation, it&nbsp;
actually does work this way. So to start this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.960
let's talk about what is a good level of&nbsp;
inflation or deflation or no price changes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:01.440
like what would we want? Well, a really high level&nbsp;
of inflation is bad because people very quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.640
can't afford things. It's bad for the economy&nbsp;
because things get so expensive that others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.480
don't buy anything. So it's bad for business.&nbsp;
It makes it so that people's wages no longer can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.680
keep up with the cost of living. So really, high&nbsp;
inflation is bad and we're seeing high inflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:24.080
at least by American historical standards right&nbsp;
now. It might seem like, well, then, is deflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.920
good? Because if prices go down, people can afford&nbsp;
stuff. No, deflation is also bad. And again, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:36.000
framework here is the framework is the traditional&nbsp;
understanding of how modern economies work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.120
You could say, David, sir, the entire framework is&nbsp;
crap. Put it in the trash and that's a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:47.520
discussion. But at least in the modern economic&nbsp;
framework. Deflation is bad because if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.800
expect things to get cheaper in the future, you&nbsp;
would wait to buy them. I mean, okay, things you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.400
absolutely need, you're not going to say, well,&nbsp;
I won't eat for a month because food will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.840
cheaper in a month. No. Then you're dead. You need&nbsp;
food, but with a ton of different purchases if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.440
expect prices to be lower in the future because&nbsp;
we are in the middle of a deflationary situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.560
you would say I'm going to wait to buy stuff.&nbsp;
And when you don't buy, businesses don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.880
money coming in, meaning they have to lay off&nbsp;
workers. And now those workers don't have money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.000
to buy things because they've been laid off. So&nbsp;
that's a deflationary spiral that's also bad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.440
So in traditional economics, the understanding&nbsp;
is you want a little bit of inflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:32.720
a little bit of inflation is where economic&nbsp;
growth comes from. A little bit of inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:38.640
is the reason why people would you're not going&nbsp;
to go hoarding supplies, but you would purchase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:45.280
things. If I want to look at a car and the car&nbsp;
is affordable today, the car is ten grand and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:51.280
can afford ten grand if I expect the car will be&nbsp;
eight grand next year. I wait. I don't buy it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:56.400
If the if the car is already unaffordable, I&nbsp;
don't buy it. But if the car is ten grand today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:02.400
and I expect it to be $10,500 in a year, that's a&nbsp;
pretty good reason to go and buy the car today. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.200
I'm now supporting the dealership. I'm supporting&nbsp;
the manufacturer. Okay. So that's the framework&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:14.160
for why we say a little bit of inflation is good.&nbsp;
Let's talk about then what happens when there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:21.280
inflation. Inflation initially creates more&nbsp;
inflation. People fear while prices are going up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:26.480
they might go even higher. I've got to go and&nbsp;
buy stuff now before I can no longer afford it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:33.440
That increases inflation even more and things&nbsp;
start to cost more for workers. So the workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.520
demand more wages and to cover the increased&nbsp;
wages, the employers start charging more for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:45.760
products and you have an inflationary cycle. This&nbsp;
only works in the short term because you can only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:51.840
pre-purchase so much stuff. You can only get&nbsp;
ahead before prices go up so long. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:58.000
inflation is going to reduce the purchasing power&nbsp;
for people. People aren't going to be able to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.320
as much. UFF A great example is like with a house,&nbsp;
if you've got a down payment saved up and you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:12.000
afford $1,000 a month mortgage at a 3% mortgage,&nbsp;
maybe your house that you can afford is 200 grand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:19.600
When mortgage rates go up, as we see inflation.&nbsp;
You can no longer afford a $200,000 house because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.480
to keep your payment at 1000 bucks because of&nbsp;
the higher rate, now you can only afford $180,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:34.160
house. This then puts pressure on the home seller&nbsp;
to decrease the price. So eventually inflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:41.600
which takes away purchasing power, starts to make&nbsp;
a downward pressure on prices. And that ultimately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:48.000
is what reduces the inflation. Now, obviously&nbsp;
there's problems with doing nothing because in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:52.800
the short and medium term, while this is going&nbsp;
on, people start to be unable to afford things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.240
that they need. Poor people are hurt the most by&nbsp;
this inflationary cycle. So you've got to figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.320
out how do we deal with that? And there's a hole,&nbsp;
but that's beyond the scope of this segment. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:06.000
there's a whole bunch of things that governments&nbsp;
can do to say, well, let's let's figure out how to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:12.720
support folks who can't afford certain necessities&nbsp;
during this period of time. High inflation can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:18.240
hurt business innovation because it starts to get&nbsp;
more expensive to start a business. Someone might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.480
say, I'm not going to start the business. Well,&nbsp;
it's a business that could have employed people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:26.560
It's a business that could have manufactured a&nbsp;
product or provided a service. So that's not good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.640
for the economy either. And you've got to figure&nbsp;
out how to deal with that. None of this is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.680
pretend that anything is any worse or better than&nbsp;
it actually is. It's to gain an understanding of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:44.240
how longer term inflation will reduce inflation.&nbsp;
In the meantime, we have to take very seriously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:48.320
the effect that it has on people, the effect that&nbsp;
it has on the economy. But for people who say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.720
well, why can't Joe Biden just like sign something&nbsp;
and fix this issue in an economy the size of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:07:02.400
economy of the United States? No, the answer is&nbsp;
no. And the mechanism that will ultimately reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:09.120
that inflation to some degree is inflation itself.&nbsp;
Let me know if that's clear or if it's not clear.

